// The real Unicode identifier tables: ID_Start and ID_Continue, exact, as the two guards
// a grammar puts behind an identifier character. SEPARATE from lib/ident.abnf on purpose -
// that module defines Id and KwEnd, and :include()ing two definitions of one production
// name into a single a-grammar is a hard error, so a grammar with its own Unicode-aware
// Id (js, typescript) cannot include it. Nothing in this file redefines Id, so it
// composes with any of them. Which languages these tables are the right answer FOR is a
// question with a surprising answer; see the block further down before adopting them.
//
// Everything here is OPT-IN, like lib/ident.abnf's IdBacktick: a grammar wires the
// productions into its own Id and pays nothing otherwise.
//
// USE: put the guard AFTER the character it judges, never in front.
//
//     Id      = IdStart :whitespace() { IdPart } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
//     IdStart = ( IdEscape IdCpStartOk ) | "a"..."z" | ... | ( IdUniChar IdCpStartOk ) ;
//     IdPart  = ( IdEscape IdCpPartOk )  | "a"..."z" | ... | ( IdUniChar IdCpPartOk ) ;
//
// The consume-then-judge order is the whole design. A guard IN FRONT of the character
// would have to skip the leading whitespace itself - :script() is not a terminal, so the
// parser skips nothing before it - and "the leading whitespace" in a real language
// includes comments, so the guards this replaced hand-skipped six ASCII characters and
// refused 'var /*c*/\u0061' as a result. Behind the character there is nothing left to
// skip, and c.peek() takes negative offsets.
//
// COST: one range attempt (IdUniChar) for every identifier position that is not an
// identifier - cheaper than the thirteen-alternative approximations this replaced, and
// far cheaper than the ~700 ABNF ranges a spelled-out table would need, which failing
// positions would re-scan every time (the found list memoizes matches only). The script
// itself runs only when a non-ASCII character or a backslash is actually there.
//
// REGENERATING the tables (they are Unicode 17.0, from the same node that is the js
// oracle, and they were verified code point by code point against \p{...} over the whole
// range 0x80-0x10FFFF):
//
//     node -e 'for (const p of ["ID_Start","ID_Continue"]) {
//       const re = new RegExp("\\p{"+p+"}","u"); let cur = 0, out = "";
//       const num = v => { const d = []; do { d.unshift(v % 16); v = Math.floor(v/16) } while (v > 0);
//         return d.map((x,i) => String.fromCharCode(48 + x + (i < d.length-1 ? 16 : 0))).join("") };
//       let a = -1;
//       for (let cp = 128; cp <= 0x110000; cp++) {
//         const ok = cp <= 0x10FFFF && !(cp >= 0xD800 && cp <= 0xDFFF) && re.test(String.fromCodePoint(cp));
//         if (ok && a < 0) a = cp; else if (!ok && a >= 0) { out += num(a-cur) + num(cp-1-a); cur = cp; a = -1 } }
//       console.log(p, out) }'
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHO MAY USE THESE TABLES, and who may not. ID_Start/ID_Continue is JavaScript's and
// TypeScript's rule; it is NOT the general "Unicode identifier" rule, and every other
// language checked here differs. Each line below was measured on this machine, not
// inferred, because assuming they are the same would tighten four grammars wrongly.
//
//   js, typescript   ID_Start / ID_Continue. Wired in. node's own parser agrees with
//                    node's \p{ID_Start} / \p{ID_Continue} on every probe, including
//                    the three that look like exceptions and are not: U+00B7, U+203F
//                    and U+200C are ID_Continue, and 'var a<U+00B7>b' parses.
//
//   swift            NOT these tables. The Swift reference gives identifier-head as an
//                    explicit list, and swiftc proves the difference in BOTH directions:
//                    'let <U+1F002> = 1' COMPILES (the list carries U+10000-U+1FFFD)
//                    while ID_Start refuses it, and 'let a<U+00A1> = 1' is rejected
//                    ("consecutive statements on a line") while our own approximation
//                    accepts it. Swift needs its own list - and it is ~45 plain ranges,
//                    so it needs no table and no guard at all.
//
//   java             NOT these tables. Character.isJavaIdentifierStart/Part is the rule,
//                    and the installed JDK 24 disagrees three ways: U+00A3 (a currency
//                    sign) STARTS a Java identifier and is not ID_Start; U+00B7 and
//                    U+0387 are ID_Continue and are not Java identifier parts; U+2118
//                    is ID_Start (Other_ID_Start) and is not a Java identifier start.
//                    java also has no Unicode identifiers here at all today - it takes
//                    the ASCII Id from lib/ident-dollar.abnf - so adopting anything is a
//                    WIDENING, not a tightening.
//
//   kotlin           NOT these tables, and untestable here (no kotlinc). The published
//                    grammar spells Identifier out of Letter = Ll|Lm|Lo|Lt|Lu|Nl and
//                    UnicodeDigit = Nd, so it has no Other_ID_Start, no Mn/Mc and no Pc.
//                    Like java it is ASCII-only here today.
//
//   c#               NOT these tables, and untestable here (no C# toolchain). ECMA-334
//                    6.4.3 builds identifier_start_character from Lu|Ll|Lt|Lm|Lo|Nl plus
//                    '_', and identifier_part_character adds Nd, Pc, Mn|Mc and ALL of
//                    Cf - so it is wider than ID_Continue on Cf and narrower on
//                    Other_ID_Start/Other_ID_Continue. Its Id here is
//                    '"\x7f"..."\U0010ffff"', which accepts a no-break space in a name;
//                    the mono corpus has no reject row, so tightening it would be
//                    unmeasurable either way.
//
//   python           XID_Start / XID_Continue, not ID_*: the language reference defines
//                    identifier ::= xid_start xid_continue*. Its tables must be generated
//                    from PYTHON, not from node - python3 3.14.6 here carries Unicode
//                    16.0 and node 24 carries 17.0, and the two disagree on 4,647 code
//                    points for XID_Start alone, so a node-generated table would be a
//                    silent oracle mismatch. Not wired in.

// One raw code point above ASCII. The bound is the whole non-ASCII range: WHICH of them
// may appear in a name is the guards' answer, not this range's, and keeping the range
// wide is what makes a failing identifier position cost one attempt instead of hundreds.
IdUniChar   = "\u0080"..."\U0010ffff" ;

